.TH WMTEST 1
.SH NAME
wmtest \- make and unmake windows, repeatedly
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wmtest
[
.B -n
.I windows
]
[
.B -d
]
[
.B -k
]
[
.B -q
]
[
.B -r
.I resultfile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Wmtest
creates windows and lets them go, a hundred times by default, and says whether
any of it failed.
It writes its verdict to
.I resultfile
.RB ( /wmtest.result
by default) as well as printing it, because
.IR wm (1)
intercepts
.B print
into its own Log window where whatever started the test cannot see it.
.PP
.B "The file is the verdict, not the exit status."
A session with
.IR wm (1)
in it does not end when this does, so whatever runs it either waits and reads
the file or kills the session \(em and killing the session takes the status
with it.
.PP
.B -q
ends the session once the verdict has been written, which buys one thing and
not another.
It buys a
.I hang
being visible: a run that finishes exits at once, so a run still going when the
timeout fires was stuck.
Without it every run is killed by the timeout and reports the same status
whether it worked or hung, which is the one failure nothing else here catches
\(em see
.IR faultprobe (1).
.PP
It does not buy pass or fail in the status.
Ending the session takes this process with it, so nothing after that runs and
no exception propagates.
The verdict is still the file.
.PP
By default it goes through
.IR tkclient (2)
toplevels, which is how a program normally gets a window.
.B -d
calls
.B Screen.newwindow
directly instead.
.B -p
makes them in that many processes at once.
.B -k
does something else again: it puts four coloured labels in one
.IR wm (1)
window, reads a pixel from each, waits, and reads them again.
Both are worth having: Tk's toplevel does a great deal besides call
.BR newwindow ,
so a failure through it does not say where the failure was.
.PP
It also asks each window its size rather than assuming it worked, since a
window that exists with no size is the same failure wearing a different hat.
.SH "WHY IT EXISTS"
.IR Wm (1)
had no automated test of any kind, which is awkward for the part of it with an
open bug:
.B Screen.newwindow
has been seen to return nil, rarely, and the report was never reproduced.
.PP
This is not a clever test.
Its value is the rate.
A person driving a session by hand makes a few windows a minute; this makes
five thousand in a run, and says so in an exit status.
.SH "WHAT IT SHOWS SO FAR"
With
.BR -k ,
four labels in a wm window keep their colours across continuous resizing: five
runs, nothing changed, and recolouring two of them between the readings reports
exactly those two, so the check has been seen to fail.
.PP
Five thousand
.B newwindow
calls and three hundred Tk toplevels in one process, and four thousand eight
hundred across sixteen processes at once, none failed.
.PP
Read that narrowly.
It says creation is clean at those counts, sequential and concurrent.
The bug it is aimed at is reported
.I after a resize ,
and nothing here resizes anything.
Something can, now:
.B INFERNO_RESIZE_TEST
(see
.IR emu (1))
resizes the window on a timer, and with it running \(em every 300ms \(em 2400
.B newwindow
calls across eight processes and 250 Tk toplevels through
.IR wm (1)
also came through with nothing failing.
.PP
That last one is the relevant number.
.B -d
allocates its own screen, because a client under
.IR wm (1)
has none to borrow, so it is not exercising wm's; the Tk path goes through
.IR tkclient (2)
to wm, which is where the reported failure lives.
.PP
The concurrent mode is the one worth running.
The fault was first characterised on a build where
.B unlock
had no release barrier, so it looked like a race, and one process making
windows one after another is close to the least likely way to provoke a race.
.SH "WINDOWS THAT EXIST ARE NOT WINDOWS THAT ARE RIGHT"
Everything else here asks whether a window was made.
.B -k
asks whether what was drawn into it is still there, which is the shape of the
resize faults this tree has actually had \(em a white block after a grow, icons
going blank \(em where nothing faults, nothing is reported, and the pixels are
simply wrong.
No amount of watching the output finds that.
.PP
The windows are
.IR wm (1)'s,
through
.IR tkclient (2),
and not a screen of this program's own.
A screen allocated on the display image does not survive a host resize, because
the image is replaced underneath it, so windows on one go uniformly
background-coloured every time.
An earlier version of this did that and reported twenty-four failures under
resizing, which looked like a discovery and was the documented consequence of
doing something unsupported.
.PP
Each widget is found again through Tk before the second reading, because a
resize is allowed to move it.
What is not allowed is for it to stop being the colour it was.
.PP
The bytes are not decoded.
A window's channel is not necessarily the one a colour was given in; the first
attempt assumed an order, got every byte reversed, and called every window
broken when none of them was.
Comparing a widget with itself needs no encoding at all.
.SH "A CLIENT MAY NOT SAY EXIT AND CARRY ON\"
.B tkclient->wmctl(t, "exit")
tells
.IR wm (1)
that the client is finished, and
.I wm
then takes the whole client away \(em not just that window.
A loop which made a window, used it, and said
.B exit
died silently on its first pass, having written nothing.
.PP
Dropping the reference to the toplevel is what unmaps one window without ending
the program.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/wmtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wm (1),
.IR nowmtest (1),
.IR clicktest (1),
.IR tkclient (2)
.PP
.B doc/hpc-plan.md
bug 2.
.SH BUGS
It does not resize anything, which is the condition the bug it was written for
is reported under.
See the plan for why that cannot be driven from inside.
.PP
Concurrent mode shares one screen between its processes, which is the
interesting case and not the only one; separate screens per process is another
test.
.PP
It leaves the last window on the screen when it finishes.
